    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Logic sits in the right units.** `DeleteOwner` owns the soft-delete (flag + save, correctly reusing `save` rather than `delete`), and each create-time check filters out deleted owners inline exactly where it already iterates `ownerRepository.findAll()`. No new catch-all class or method appears; nothing is relocated awkwardly.\n\n- **The repeated `Boolean.TRUE.equals(getDeleted())` predicate matches existing precedent.** The codebase already treats nullable `Boolean` flags this way inline \u2014 `FlagPossibleDuplicate` and `RejectDuplicateIdentity` both already use `Boolean.TRUE.equals(sharesHousehold)` without a shared helper. Each of the four check classes independently calls `findAll()` and filters locally (two via stream, two via loop `continue`); adding the deleted filter inline follows that established shape. Extracting an `Owner.isDeleted()` helper or a shared \"active owners\" filter would be a reasonable alternative, but given the entity is a plain JavaBean (every flag exposed as a bare getter/setter) and the null-safe idiom is already duplicated for `sharesHousehold`, consolidating only `deleted` would actually be *less* consistent with current conventions. The one-liner-per-site duplication here is below the threshold where a maintainer of this code would reach for an abstraction.\n\n- **The entity getter/setter addition matches the surrounding bean style** (cf. `possibleDuplicate`/`possibleDuplicateOf`), and the field default `Boolean.FALSE` aligns with the \"newly created owner is false\" requirement.\n\n- **Cross-cutting artifacts are kept in step:** all four schema dialects, the JPA column, and the OpenAPI `readOnly` field are updated together, and `DeleteOwner`'s new javadoc cross-references the create-check behavior so the soft-delete contract is discoverable from the delete step.\n\nNo structural changes needed.",
